import{at as e,it as t,o as n,vt as r}from"./chunks/framework.DQt_9H6f.js";var i=JSON.parse(`{"title":"Tailoring TeXRA","description":"","frontmatter":{},"headers":[],"relativePath":"guide/customizing.md","filePath":"guide/customizing.md"}`),a={name:`guide/customizing.md`};function o(n,i,a,o,s,c){return r(),t(`div`,null,[...i[0]||=[e(`<h1 id="tailoring-texra" tabindex="-1">Tailoring TeXRA <a class="header-anchor" href="#tailoring-texra" aria-label="Permalink to “Tailoring TeXRA”">​</a></h1><p>The assistant&#39;s own prompt and tools are fixed. You shape what it does in a project with the things below; none of them replaces the assistant, they add to it.</p><h2 id="project-instructions-agents-md" tabindex="-1">Project instructions: <code>AGENTS.md</code> <a class="header-anchor" href="#project-instructions-agents-md" aria-label="Permalink to “Project instructions: AGENTS.md”">​</a></h2><p>Put the rules every conversation in a project should follow (notation, macros, journal style, how to build the paper, files never to touch) in an <code>AGENTS.md</code> file at the workspace root. TeXRA adds it to the assistant&#39;s instructions in every conversation. Read <a href="./configuration.html#project-instructions-agentsmd">Project instructions</a>.</p><h2 id="memory" tabindex="-1">Memory <a class="header-anchor" href="#memory" aria-label="Permalink to “Memory”">​</a></h2><p>The assistant can keep notes about the project in its memory and read them in later conversations. Read <a href="./memory.html">Memory</a>.</p><h2 id="plugins-on-and-off" tabindex="-1">Plugins on and off <a class="header-anchor" href="#plugins-on-and-off" aria-label="Permalink to “Plugins on and off”">​</a></h2><p><strong>Settings → Plugins</strong> (or <code>/plugins</code> in the terminal chat) switches each group of tools on or off: web search, Lean, Wolfram, the Codex and Claude Code integrations, multi-agent work and more. A plugin that is off adds nothing to the conversation.</p><h2 id="skills" tabindex="-1">Skills <a class="header-anchor" href="#skills" aria-label="Permalink to “Skills”">​</a></h2><p>A skill is a folder with a <code>SKILL.md</code> that tells the assistant how to do one kind of work. TeXRA ships four of its own (<code>arxiv</code> and <code>zotero</code>, read <a href="./research-tools.html">Research tools</a>; <code>github</code>, read <a href="./agent-integrations.html">Agent integrations</a>; <code>plugin-authoring</code>, for writing a plugin with a native <code>harness.mjs</code>) and reads more from installed plugins, from <code>.texra/skills/</code> in the project, from your own skills folder, and, once you switch that source on, from the <code>.agents</code>, <code>.claude</code>, <code>.codex</code> and <code>.gemini</code> skill folders beside the project and in your home folder. Turn skills on with <strong>Enable skills for agents</strong> in the <strong>Skills</strong> section of <strong>Settings → Agents</strong>, where you also choose the sources. In the terminal, <code>texra skills list</code> shows them and <code>/skills</code> applies one to your next request. Read <a href="./texra-cli.html#skills">Skills in the CLI guide</a>.</p><h2 id="install-a-plugin" tabindex="-1">Install a plugin <a class="header-anchor" href="#install-a-plugin" aria-label="Permalink to “Install a plugin”">​</a></h2><p>TeXRA installs plugins published for Claude Code and Codex. A plugin can bring skills, commands, agents, MCP servers, programs (<code>programs/&lt;name&gt;.js</code>), and native TeXRA extensions (<code>harness.mjs</code>):</p><div class="language-bash"><button title="Copy Code" class="copy"></button><span class="lang">bash</span><pre class="shiki shiki-themes github-light github-dark" style="--shiki-light:#24292e;--shiki-dark:#e1e4e8;--shiki-light-bg:#fff;--shiki-dark-bg:#24292e;" tabindex="0" dir="ltr"><code><span class="line"><span style="--shiki-light:#6F42C1;--shiki-dark:#B392F0;">texra</span><span style="--shiki-light:#032F62;--shiki-dark:#9ECBFF;"> plugin</span><span style="--shiki-light:#032F62;--shiki-dark:#9ECBFF;"> install</span><span style="--shiki-light:#032F62;--shiki-dark:#9ECBFF;"> github.com/</span><span style="--shiki-light:#c62739;--shiki-dark:#F97583;">&lt;</span><span style="--shiki-light:#032F62;--shiki-dark:#9ECBFF;">owne</span><span style="--shiki-light:#24292E;--shiki-dark:#E1E4E8;">r</span><span style="--shiki-light:#c62739;--shiki-dark:#F97583;">&gt;</span><span style="--shiki-light:#032F62;--shiki-dark:#9ECBFF;">/</span><span style="--shiki-light:#c62739;--shiki-dark:#F97583;">&lt;</span><span style="--shiki-light:#032F62;--shiki-dark:#9ECBFF;">rep</span><span style="--shiki-light:#24292E;--shiki-dark:#E1E4E8;">o</span><span style="--shiki-light:#c62739;--shiki-dark:#F97583;">&gt;</span></span>
<span class="line"><span style="--shiki-light:#6F42C1;--shiki-dark:#B392F0;">texra</span><span style="--shiki-light:#032F62;--shiki-dark:#9ECBFF;"> plugin</span><span style="--shiki-light:#032F62;--shiki-dark:#9ECBFF;"> enable</span><span style="--shiki-light:#c62739;--shiki-dark:#F97583;"> &lt;</span><span style="--shiki-light:#032F62;--shiki-dark:#9ECBFF;">nam</span><span style="--shiki-light:#24292E;--shiki-dark:#E1E4E8;">e</span><span style="--shiki-light:#c62739;--shiki-dark:#F97583;">&gt;</span></span></code></pre></div><p>An installed plugin stays off until you review and trust what it carries (a native <code>harness.mjs</code> entry loads from a snapshot of the trusted files, and any edit requires re-trusting). In the terminal chat you can also use <code>/plugins add</code>, and in the VS Code extension and desktop app manage installed plugins under <strong>Settings → Plugins</strong>. Read <a href="./texra-cli.html#plugins">Plugins in the CLI guide</a>.</p><p>A plugin&#39;s agents are Claude Code subagent files (<code>agents/&lt;name&gt;.md</code>: a name, a description and an optional tool list, with the agent&#39;s instructions as the body). While the plugin is enabled, the assistant is told of each one as <code>&lt;plugin&gt;:&lt;name&gt;</code> and can hand it a piece of work by that name, so ask for it: &quot;have <code>my-plugin:referee</code> read section 3&quot;. An agent that lists no tools gets the file, shell and web tools, the tools every agent gets injected (<code>plan</code>, <code>script</code>, <code>context</code>, and the rest), and the tools of the plugins you have on.</p><h2 id="write-your-own-program" tabindex="-1">Write your own program <a class="header-anchor" href="#write-your-own-program" aria-label="Permalink to “Write your own program”">​</a></h2><p>A program is a JavaScript file that runs a fixed procedure over the files you give it. Put it in a plugin&#39;s <code>programs/</code> folder and it is started as <code>&lt;plugin&gt;:&lt;name&gt;</code>, by you (<code>texra run &lt;plugin&gt;:&lt;name&gt; --input …</code>) or by the assistant when you ask for it. Read <a href="./texra-cli.html#programs-in-a-plugin">Programs in a plugin</a>.</p><p>The assistant can also write a program for itself, saved in the project&#39;s memory, and start it again in later conversations. Read <a href="./texra-cli.html#programs-an-agent-writes-for-itself">Programs an agent writes for itself</a>.</p><h2 id="mcp-servers" tabindex="-1">MCP servers <a class="header-anchor" href="#mcp-servers" aria-label="Permalink to “MCP servers”">​</a></h2><p>The Model Context Protocol servers an installed plugin carries give the assistant their tools while the plugin is enabled. Read <a href="./agent-integrations.html#mcp-servers">MCP servers</a>.</p><h2 id="a-different-point-of-view-is-a-sentence" tabindex="-1">A different point of view is a sentence <a class="header-anchor" href="#a-different-point-of-view-is-a-sentence" aria-label="Permalink to “A different point of view is a sentence”">​</a></h2><p>You need no new agent to change how a job is done. The programs take their scope and point of view from the instruction: &quot;read as a referee for a physics journal and mark every unsupported claim&quot; is <code>annotate</code> with that sentence. To combine steps, ask the assistant, for example to run <code>annotate</code> and then fix what it flagged.</p><div class="info custom-block"><p class="custom-block-title">If you used 0.40</p><p>Custom agent YAML files, the custom agents folder, the Agents library in Settings, and customizing a built-in agent are gone. Move an agent&#39;s standing rules into <code>AGENTS.md</code>, a checklist into a skill, and a repeatable procedure into a plugin program. The skill collections listed on the <a href="./open-source.html">Open source</a> page are published as Claude Code plugins.</p></div>`,23)]])}var s=n(a,[[`render`,o]]);export{i as __pageData,s as default};